<h2>The transfer plea</h2>
<p>The husband&rsquo;s application invoked Section 24 read with Section 151 CPC, seeking transfer of DV Case No. 139/2025 — the wife&rsquo;s application under Section 12 of the 2005 Act — from the Judicial Magistrate First Class, Gurugram to the Family Court, Gurugram. The logic was familiar: the same spouses, overlapping allegations, common evidence, and the spectre of two courts reaching inconsistent findings on the same marriage. Since Section 26 of the DV Act allows DV reliefs to be granted by a Family Court, why not let one judge hear everything?</p>

<h2>The statutory architecture</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Provision</th><th>What it actually does</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 12, DV Act</td><td>The aggrieved person&rsquo;s substantive application for reliefs — presented to the Magistrate.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 27, DV Act</td><td>Designates the Judicial Magistrate First Class (or Metropolitan Magistrate) as the competent court for Section 12 applications.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 26, DV Act</td><td>Permits reliefs under Sections 18&ndash;22 to be sought <em>in addition</em>, in any legal proceeding already pending before a civil court, Family Court or criminal court.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 7(2)(a), Family Courts Act</td><td>Gives Family Courts the jurisdiction of a Magistrate under Chapter IX CrPC — maintenance — and nothing wider on the criminal side.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What the court held</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Pendency confers no jurisdiction.</strong> The mere pendency of a maintenance petition before the Family Court, the court held, &ldquo;does not confer upon that Court the jurisdiction to entertain&rdquo; an independent application under Section 12. The DV Act chose its forum deliberately.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Section 26 is additive, not original.</strong> The provision lets a litigant stack DV reliefs onto proceedings that already exist; it &ldquo;cannot be interpreted so as to enlarge the jurisdiction of the Family Court&rdquo; into a forum of first instance for DV complaints.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Convenience is not a ground.</strong> &ldquo;The mere fact that the parties are common or that certain allegations may overlap cannot be a ground to transfer&rdquo; — and jurisdiction &ldquo;cannot be assumed merely on the basis of convenience.&rdquo; The parties were directed to appear before the Magistrate on the date already fixed.</div>
</div>
<p>The order aligned itself with the Kerala Division Bench and Delhi High Court decisions taking the same view of Section 26, declined to follow the contrary Bombay approach, and discussed the Supreme Court&rsquo;s 2025 exposition of the DV Act&rsquo;s scheme (<em>Shaurabh Kumar Tripathi v. Vidhi Rawal</em>, 2025 INSC 734) — all as recorded in the order itself.</p>

<h2>Why this matters in daily practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Forum-shopping by transfer is closed.</strong> Respondents who find the Magistrate&rsquo;s summary timeline uncomfortable cannot decant the case into the Family Court&rsquo;s slower civil rhythm by pointing to a pending maintenance petition.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Section 26 remains a claimant&rsquo;s tool.</strong> The aggrieved person may choose to seek DV reliefs inside a pending divorce or maintenance case — the election belongs to her, not to the respondent seeking consolidation.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Parallel proceedings are the norm.</strong> DV, maintenance, divorce and criminal complaints routinely run side by side. The answer to multiplicity is disciplined disclosure and adjustment of overlapping monetary reliefs, not forced consolidation.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Delhi practice mirrors this.</strong> The Delhi High Court authority followed in the order means the same transfer strategy faces the same fate in Delhi&rsquo;s districts, where Mahila Courts hear Section 12 applications.</div>
</div>

<div class="note">The decision does not deny that fragmentation burdens families; it holds that the cure lies with the legislature, not with transfer applications. Until then, the Section 12 case belongs to the Magistrate — and litigants on both sides are better served by coordinating the parallel files than by litigating over which courtroom they sit in.</div>
